  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218283/assets/82012efd1fbf78238ce1b6ecc49819283976db8f39ac5f57ec232a5eda8029dc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above and the point $(0,1)$ lies on the curve. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x)+2?$",
  "worked_solution": "The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $(0,1)$ as shown in the diagram. \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)+2$ can be obtained from $y=f(x)$ by translating it by $2$ units *up*. So, it must pass through the point $(0,3).$\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218283/assets/5f0a46e8f6ef1daac4acbaf49e5fb1ffc21046cbfc6260fe6dbe51d1814a1c12.png)",
